:name: HWA10 update path
:description: The WPP pipe run with the boot chain that applies a pending bank:
:  the MBR hands control to the bootloader at 0xfc000, which copies the bank the
:  application downloaded into internal flash, and the application is re-patched
:  on the way in.

include @machine.resc

# UICR.NRFFW[0] holds the bootloader start address the MBR reads, and boot.resc
# leaves it erased so the MBR forwards to the SoftDevice. This run arms it only
# for the reboot that has a bank to apply: the bootloader compares the whole
# 1.15 MB bank against internal flash one SPI byte at a time, so taking that
# path on every boot costs minutes per boot, and the sim's own patches make
# internal flash differ from the bank by construction, so the comparison would
# never settle.

# CMD_RESTART_TO_UPDATE's handler is the moment the application asks for the
# bank to be applied: arm the hand-off so the reboot it is about to cause lands
# in the bootloader.
cpu AddHook $hook_restart_to_update 'machine.SystemBus.WriteDoubleWord(0x10001014, 0xfc000)'

# The bootloader's reset entry. It reads POWER.RESETREAS to decide whether the
# RAM control block the application filled in survived; the sim has no reset
# cause to report, and every reset that reaches the bootloader here came from
# SYSRESETREQ, which is bit 2.
cpu AddHook $hook_bootloader_reset 'monitor.Parse("sysbus.clock ResetReason 4")'

runMacro $fastStartup

logLevel -1 sysbus.wppPipe
emulation SetAdvanceImmediately true
sysbus.wppPipe Attach $hook_ble_evt_present $hook_ble_evt_poll $hook_hvx_thunk

# From here on the addresses belong to the image the update installs, not to the
# one this run started from, so the rig abi/rig.py generated for it redefines
# every macro and variable above. The two are the same file for a run that
# installs the image it booted, and different for one that installs a relinked
# or relaid-out image.
include @out/rig-updated/patches.resc
include @out/rig-updated/hooks.resc

macro postUpdate
"""
    runMacro $imagePatches
    runMacro $fastStartup
    sysbus.wppPipe Attach $hook_ble_evt_present $hook_ble_evt_poll $hook_hvx_thunk
    # 0x20000000 is where the MBR keeps the vector table it forwards SVCs and
    # interrupts to. Launching the bootloader pointed it at the bootloader's own
    # table and the bootloader jumps to the application without putting it back,
    # so the first SVC -- the one that starts the FreeRTOS scheduler -- lands in
    # the bootloader and the application never runs a task. Point it at the
    # SoftDevice, which is what the MBR leaves behind when it starts the
    # SoftDevice itself.
    sysbus WriteDoubleWord 0x20000000 0x1000
    sysbus.nvmc SnapshotProtectedRegion 0x0 0x27000
    sysbus WriteDoubleWord 0x10001014 0xFFFFFFFF
"""

# The application's reset entry, in the image the update installs. The
# bootloader has just overwritten internal flash with the bank, so the sim's
# patches are gone and the protected-region reference is stale; postUpdate
# restores both and disarms the hand-off so the next ordinary reboot goes
# straight to the SoftDevice.
#
# The address belongs to the installed image, so under a layout that moved the
# text it is an ordinary instruction of the image this run booted from, and that
# one executes it on the way up. UICR.NRFFW[0] is what tells the two apart: only
# the reboot the application asked for leaves the bootloader armed, and
# postUpdate disarms it again.
cpu AddHook $hook_app_reset """
if cpu.GetMachine().SystemBus.ReadDoubleWord(0x10001014)==0xfc000:
    monitor.Parse("runMacro $postUpdate")
"""

start
